//@ compile-fail
//@ error-contains: both answer for
//@ error-contains: two impls for the same target are not nested

// Two impls of one protocol for the SAME target. Neither is more specific, so there is no
// most-specific one to dispatch to, and `most_specific` in `dispatch.rs` has no answer.
//
// This used to check clean. `check_overlap` permits nesting -- the narrower target wins at
// every value they share -- and spelled that as `a <: b || b <: a`, which is true of two
// EQUAL targets: they are mutual subtypes, so a duplicate read as "nested" and was waved
// through. In the same module, with no diagnostic.
//
// It failed later and elsewhere. `collect_impl_bodies` keys bodies by
// `protocol$head$method`, so the two impls collide on one key: one body wins the slot and
// the other's call sites reach a `<todo: ..>` marker, or the wrong body. That is a C
// compiler error or a wrong answer, neither of which names the duplicate.
//
// Nesting is now required to be STRICT, which is what the rule always meant.

use std::io;

record B { n: i64 }

protocol Sz for T {
    fn sz(v: T) -> i64
}

impl Sz for B {
    fn sz(v: B) -> i64 { 1 }
}

impl Sz for B {
    fn sz(v: B) -> i64 { 2 }
}

fn main() {
    io::println(to_string(sz(B { n: 0 })));
}
